Why are you doing this?  It's fun, and it's useful for pacifists.  It's
also worth doing several times for everyone to learn the skills
involved.  But it's not a high-percentage strategy for a regular game
(essentially you're trading making the first 8 or so dungeon levels
much more dangerous in exchange for making 7 of the next 10 or so
somewhat less dangerous; after that point you'll probably have full
protection even if you didn't do the racket.).

> Well, I'm not getting why
> when I engrave the E-word with my fingers, monsters frequently get in
> hits.  What is up?

Just to pull together advice from the thread:
1. When you go to write E w/ your fingers, you may mess up
2. If you move or attack while standing on E, you can mess it up
(probably _will_ mess it up for finger-E).
3. It can wear off over time.

Some things that can help:
1. If you're not planning on attacking, just standing there safely,
write E every turn (adding to the existing writing).  It's not uncommon
to have it written 10+ times in one spot.
2. Levitating over E makes fighting less likely to smudge it.
3. Casting spells or throwing weapons off of E
4. Wands of fire and lightning inscribe permanently, so using their
charges for E is usually much more valuable than zapping at monsters.
Permanently means that you can fight all you want while standing there.
5. Wands of digging and athames (including Magicbane, the guaranteed
first sac gift for wizards) inscripe semi-permanently; it's not
permanent, but much more durable than finger-dust writing and usually
lasts for a long, long time unless you're moving or fighting on it.
6. Hard gems (diamond, sapphire, ruby, emerald, etc) or rings with hard
gems (diamond ring, etc) also engrave semi-permanently, as do edged
weapons (but they get dulled in the process), but all of those take
multiple turns during which you're incapacitated and monsters can just
beat on you.  Fingers are usually preferred in emergencies, but gems
are okay for, say, prepping the up stairs on a level where you know
you're going to wake a teleporting baddie, or putting an E somewhere to
guard a stash, etc (things where you aren't writing while in peril).



